update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Describe the main stages of aerobic respiration.</li><li>Explain the roles of glucose, oxygen and mitochondria.</li><li>Compare aerobic and anaerobic respiration.</li></ul><h2>Why cells respire</h2><p>Cellular respiration is a series of enzyme-controlled reactions that transfer energy from organic molecules into ATP. Cells use ATP to power processes such as active transport, biosynthesis, movement and cell division. Respiration occurs in living cells continuously, although its rate varies with activity and conditions.</p><h2>Glycolysis</h2><p>Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm. One glucose molecule is converted into two molecules of pyruvate. The pathway produces a small net gain of ATP and reduced NAD. It does not directly require oxygen, so it can occur when oxygen availability is limited.</p><h2>Aerobic respiration</h2><p>When oxygen is available and the cell has the required machinery, pyruvate is transported into mitochondria and processed further. The link reaction produces acetyl-CoA, which enters the Krebs cycle. Carbon dioxide is released, and reduced coenzymes carry high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electron transfer supports proton pumping; the resulting proton gradient drives ATP synthase. Oxygen acts as the final electron acceptor and combines with electrons and protons to form water.</p><h2>Anaerobic pathways</h2><p>When oxygen supply or use is insufficient, cells may regenerate NAD so glycolysis can continue. In human muscle, pyruvate can be reduced to lactate. In yeast, fermentation produces ethanol and carbon dioxide. These pathways yield far less ATP per glucose than complete aerobic oxidation because much of the chemical energy remains in the end products.</p><h2>Comparing energy yield</h2><p>Glycolysis yields a net two ATP per glucose directly. Aerobic respiration produces substantially more ATP overall through oxidative phosphorylation, but the exact total depends on the cell and how energy is transferred. Avoid treating a single fixed ATP number as universal across all organisms and conditions.</p><h2>Investigating respiration</h2><p>Germinating seeds can be used to investigate respiration. A respirometer can measure oxygen uptake if temperature is controlled and carbon dioxide produced is absorbed by a suitable absorbent. A control with non-germinating material helps account for changes caused by temperature or pressure rather than respiration. Follow laboratory safety instructions when using absorbents or sealed apparatus.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Respiration transfers energy from food molecules to ATP.</li><li>Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm; aerobic stages involve mitochondria in eukaryotic cells.</li><li>Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration.</li><li>Anaerobic pathways regenerate NAD but release much less energy per glucose.</li></ul>',
content_preview='Follow glucose breakdown through glycolysis, mitochondrial aerobic respiration, ATP production and anaerobic pathways.',
word_count=480,read_time='4 min read',
metadata=coalesce(metadata,'{}'::jsonb)||'{"level":"O Level","group":"Form 2","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Describe respiration stages","Compare aerobic and anaerobic pathways","Explain ATP production"]}'::jsonb,
updated_at=now()
where slug='cellular-respiration';

update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain why organisms are classified.</li><li>Use shared characteristics to compare groups.</li><li>Distinguish classification, identification and evolutionary relationships.</li></ul><h2>Why classify living organisms?</h2><p>Biological classification organises the diversity of life into groups based on shared characteristics and evidence of relationships. A consistent system helps scientists identify organisms, communicate clearly and compare findings. Common names can vary between places, so scientific names provide a more standard reference.</p><h2>Taxonomic hierarchy</h2><p>In the widely used hierarchy, organisms are grouped into domain, kingdom, phylum (or division in some plant contexts), class, order, family, genus and species. Each lower rank generally contains organisms sharing more characteristics. A species is often introduced as a group of organisms that can interbreed naturally to produce fertile offspring, but this definition has limitations for asexual organisms, fossils and some cases of hybridisation.</p><h2>Scientific naming</h2><p>Binomial nomenclature gives each species a two-part name: genus followed by specific epithet. The genus begins with a capital letter, while the specific epithet begins with a lower-case letter; both are italicised in printed text, for example <em>Homo sapiens</em>. After first use, the genus may be abbreviated when the meaning remains clear.</p><h2>Evidence used in classification</h2><p>Observable features such as body structure, reproductive traits and cell organisation can help identify groups. Modern classification also uses molecular evidence, including DNA and protein comparisons. Similarity alone does not always prove close relationship: some organisms evolve similar adaptations independently under similar environmental pressures, a process called convergent evolution.</p><h2>Cladograms and common ancestry</h2><p>A cladogram represents a hypothesis about branching evolutionary relationships. A branch point indicates a common ancestor for the lineages arising from it. Shared derived characteristics can help identify groups that descend from a common ancestor. The order of branches matters more than how close the labels appear on a page; rotating branches around a node does not change the relationships shown.</p><h2>How to classify an unknown specimen</h2><p>Record observable features carefully, use an appropriate identification key, compare several characteristics rather than one superficial feature, and check the final identification against reliable reference material. If evidence is incomplete, report the uncertainty rather than claiming a definite species.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Classification makes biological diversity easier to study and communicate.</li><li>Scientific names reduce ambiguity from local common names.</li><li>Modern classification considers evolutionary evidence, including molecular data.</li><li>A classification is a scientific hypothesis that can change when evidence improves.</li></ul>',
content_preview='Learn taxonomic ranks, binomial nomenclature, classification evidence and how to interpret evolutionary relationships.',
word_count=430,read_time='4 min read',
metadata=coalesce(metadata,'{}'::jsonb)||'{"level":"O Level","group":"Form 1","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Describe taxonomic hierarchy","Apply binomial nomenclature","Interpret classification evidence"]}'::jsonb,
updated_at=now()
where slug='classification-of-living-organisms';

